From: sokkycakes <6889324+sokkycakes@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 04:28:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] soc/amd/cyan_skillfish: Add early support for AMD Cyan
 Skillfish

Cyan Skillfish is the cut-down PS5 APU used on the ASRock BC-250. AMD's
AGESA calls it RBN and its modules Ariel. It pairs Zen 2 cores with a
Raven/Picasso style NBIO and SMU v10.

Neither FSP nor openSIL exists for this SoC. Like Picasso, though, its
PSP trains DRAM (GDDR6 here) with the ABL blobs and copies the x86 reset
image into DRAM, so the common non-CAR boot flow is reused unchanged.
This starts from the Picasso code with everything FSP-specific removed:

- PSP blobs, APCB and VBIOS come from the stock BIOS, extracted into
  3rdparty/bc250_blobs. The PSP directory mirrors the stock one entry
  for entry, and the reset image is stored uncompressed like the
  stock one.
- cbmem_top() comes from TOP_MEM, and DRAM above 4 GiB from TOP_MEM2,
  both programmed by the ABL.
- Silicon init after memory training (what AGESA's NBIO, fabric, CCX
  and FCH modules do), PCIe lane descriptors and PCI IRQ routing are
  left as TODOs. Only devices at fixed addresses are listed in the
  chipset devicetree.

